WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.158
Three databases, one in Frankfurt, one in Virginia, one in Asia.

2
00:00:07.158 --> 00:00:08.900
They all copy each other.

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:10.553
Every message arrived.

4
00:00:10.553 --> 00:00:11.676
Nothing was lost.

5
00:00:11.676 --> 00:00:19.339
And yet, for one card payment, two of them say captured and the third says authorized, and nothing will ever fix it.

6
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:23.155
How do three careful copies end up disagreeing forever?

7
00:00:23.633 --> 00:00:25.071
Here's how we got here.

8
00:00:25.071 --> 00:00:30.132
A leader is the one database that takes writes and puts them in order in its log.

9
00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:35.994
With customers on three continents, one leader means every card tap crosses an ocean.

10
00:00:35.994 --> 00:00:38.325
So each region gets its own leader.

11
00:00:38.533 --> 00:00:44.065
It saves its own customers' writes at once, and sends its log to the others a moment later.

12
00:00:44.267 --> 00:00:47.936
Picture cafés that each keep a copy of the same notebook.

13
00:00:47.936 --> 00:00:54.051
Each café writes new lines in its own copy, and phones the others its new lines a little later.

14
00:00:54.233 --> 00:01:00.021
The notebooks are the databases, each café is a leader, and the phone calls carry the logs.

15
00:01:00.233 --> 00:01:02.471
With three cafés, who calls whom?

16
00:01:02.471 --> 00:01:05.793
In a circle, each one passes changes to the next.

17
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.441
In a star, one hub hears everything and passes it on.

18
00:01:09.633 --> 00:01:13.459
Both forward, so a change could come home and go round forever.

19
00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:20.905
So every change carries its origin, where it was first written, and a café drops a change it has already applied.

20
00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:26.909
Circles and stars depend on single cafés: lose the hub, and nobody hears anything.

21
00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:31.199
So all-to-all is the usual choice: everyone calls everyone.

22
00:01:31.199 --> 00:01:32.853
Its weakness is quieter.

23
00:01:32.853 --> 00:01:35.196
The lines run at different speeds.

24
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.175
Two words for a card payment.

25
00:01:37.175 --> 00:01:41.826
When you tap, your bank sets the money aside, and the payment is authorized.

26
00:01:41.826 --> 00:01:44.764
Later the money really moves, and it's captured.

27
00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:53.772
At 0 ms, Frankfurt inserts payment pay-81723 as authorized, and sends the insert to both others.

28
00:01:53.967 --> 00:01:58.639
Virginia gets it at 40 ms, and captures the payment at 45.

29
00:01:58.639 --> 00:02:00.572
It sends that update on.

30
00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:03.976
Asia's line from Virginia takes 30 ms.

31
00:02:03.976 --> 00:02:06.847
Its line from Frankfurt takes 150.

32
00:02:06.847 --> 00:02:09.127
So what reaches Asia first?

33
00:02:09.127 --> 00:02:10.479
Pause and guess.

34
00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:18.507
The update, at 75 ms, for a row Asia doesn't have yet.

35
00:02:18.733 --> 00:02:29.300
pgactive, a free add-on from Amazon that lets several PostgreSQL servers all take writes, documents its default for this case: throw the update away.

36
00:02:29.467 --> 00:02:31.517
The insert lands at 150 ms.

37
00:02:31.517 --> 00:02:38.653
Asia says authorized, the others say captured, and no message is left on its way to repair it.

38
00:02:38.867 --> 00:02:44.146
It's a call saying change that order to blue, about an order nobody has heard of yet.

39
00:02:44.146 --> 00:02:46.133
So the café shrugs, and bins it.

40
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:48.549
You may be thinking: timestamps.

41
00:02:48.549 --> 00:02:52.203
Every leader keeps whichever version carries the later time.

42
00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.428
But say Virginia's clock runs 60 ms behind Frankfurt's.

43
00:02:56.428 --> 00:03:00.456
Then the capture is stamped minus 15, and the insert 0.

44
00:03:00.633 --> 00:03:05.976
Every region keeps the insert, Virginia included, so it ignores its own capture.

45
00:03:05.976 --> 00:03:08.113
They agree, on the wrong answer.

46
00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:11.976
A clock is a very confident witness, and that's all it is.

47
00:03:12.367 --> 00:03:16.789
What works is to carry what each change had already seen, instead of the time.

48
00:03:16.967 --> 00:03:22.200
Back at the cafés, Virginia's call starts: I've already heard Frankfurt's line 1.

49
00:03:22.433 --> 00:03:26.131
Asia hasn't heard it yet, so it holds the update, and waits.

50
00:03:26.333 --> 00:03:28.742
At 150 ms, the insert arrives.

51
00:03:28.742 --> 00:03:34.603
Asia applies it, sees the held update is now ready, and applies that too.

52
00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.849
All three say captured, whatever the clocks read.

53
00:03:37.849 --> 00:03:41.832
That record, one counter per leader, is called a version vector.

54
00:03:42.033 --> 00:03:45.176
So how did three careful copies disagree forever?

55
00:03:45.176 --> 00:03:48.703
A later change took a faster line, and got thrown away.

56
00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:52.171
Carry what each change had seen, not what time it was.

57
00:03:52.667 --> 00:03:56.386
One limit: holding works because the update had seen the insert.

58
00:03:56.386 --> 00:04:00.744
Two writes that never saw each other are a harder problem, and that's next.

59
00:04:00.933 --> 00:04:02.193
One quick check.

60
00:04:02.193 --> 00:04:05.578
An update says: needs Frankfurt's change 2.

61
00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:08.018
Asia has applied only change 1.

62
00:04:08.018 --> 00:04:09.435
What does Asia do?

63
00:04:09.435 --> 00:04:10.695
Pause and think.

64
00:04:13.867 --> 00:04:17.585
It holds the update, until Frankfurt's change 2 arrives.
